-<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Study notes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../assets/worksheet.css"><main class="shell"><h1>Ohio Constitution and Citizenship — Introduction Study Notes</h1><section class="question-panel" id="q01"><h2>CS 16 · 1</h2><p>Popular sovereignty means the people are the source of legitimate governmental power.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q03"><h2>CS 16 · 3</h2><p>Checks and balances give branches ways to limit or respond to other branches.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q04"><h2>CS 16 · 4</h2><p>Limited government subjects officials and institutions to constitutional limits.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q06"><h2>CS 16 · 6</h2><p>Elections allow the people to exercise political authority through representatives.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q13"><h2>CS 16 · 13</h2><p>Petition signatures help qualify a measure for ballot consideration; they do not decide the vote.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q15"><h2>CS 16 · 15</h2><p>Ohio imposes term limits on state legislators; the U.S. Constitution does not impose them on Congress.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q17"><h2>CS 16 · 17</h2><p>Both systems divide governmental responsibilities among three branches.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q20"><h2>CS 16 · 20</h2><p>State constitutions establish state institutions and protections subject to the U.S. Constitution.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q26"><h2>CS 16 · 26</h2><p>Constitutions provide the governing framework and limit ordinary legislation.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q28"><h2>CS 16 · 28</h2><p>Limited government means officials are bound by the law and constitution.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q29"><h2>CS 17 · 29</h2><p>Voting helps citizens select leaders and decide issues submitted to voters.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q30"><h2>CS 17 · 30</h2><p>Constituents can communicate with lawmakers during the legislative process.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q31"><h2>CS 17 · 31</h2><p>Local meetings offer opportunities to bring concerns and solutions to officials.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q32"><h2>CS 17 · 32</h2><p>School board meetings allow residents to follow local education governance and participate appropriately.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q33"><h2>CS 17 · 33</h2><p>Community volunteer work can support local public services and goals.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q34"><h2>CS 17 · 34</h2><p>Neighborhood watch programs can help residents cooperate with local public safety efforts.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q35"><h2>CS 17 · 35</h2><p>Civic participation includes hearings, committees, and other lawful public activities.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q36"><h2>CS 17 · 36</h2><p>Informed participation begins with understanding the policy and available evidence.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q38"><h2>CS 17 · 38</h2><p>Eligible residents can serve in the Ohio National Guard as a form of public service.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q39"><h2>CS 17 · 39</h2><p>Ohio election rules include petition and nomination processes for ballot access.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="q40"><h2>CS 17 · 40</h2><p>Attending and offering practical input helps local officials consider residents’ needs.</p></section></main></html>
\ No newline at end of file
