-<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Public policy reference</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../assets/worksheet.css"><main class="shell"><h1>Public Policy Reference</h1><section class="question-panel" id="sampling"><h2>Who the evidence represents</h2><p>Hearing testimony describes the experiences of the people who attended. Broader outreach and representative evidence can reveal who was missed.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="tradeoffs"><h2>Comparing policy costs and benefits</h2><p>A policy can improve one outcome while creating costs elsewhere. Compare alternatives and assess effects on each group, including public revenue, affordability, and access.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="comments"><h2>Input before a rule takes effect</h2><p>When an agency requests public comments on a proposed rule, participants can submit evidence and recommend specific changes. The agency evaluates relevant evidence within its legal authority.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="evaluation"><h2>Outputs and outcomes</h2><p>Outputs count activity, such as riders served, loans approved, or participants enrolled. Outcomes measure whether the underlying goal improved. Use a baseline and compare results for affected groups.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="effects"><h2>Intended and unintended effects</h2><p>An intended effect is part of the stated policy goal. A change outside that goal is unintended. Monitor spillovers into nearby places and changes for different groups.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="authority"><h2>Authority and implementation</h2><p>A proposal states an intended action. Implementation requires authority, funding, and the people or procedures that carry it out. A court ruling can change how an existing law is applied.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="collaboration"><h2>Problems that cross borders</h2><p>Neighboring jurisdictions can share monitoring data and coordinate standards when actions in one area affect people elsewhere.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="participation"><h2>Taking evidence to the decision maker</h2><p>Public testimony is most useful when it reaches the body responsible for the decision while changes are still possible. Documents and specific remedies help officials evaluate a request.</p></section><section class="question-panel" id="safety"><h2>Choosing a safety investment</h2><p>Compare crash history, existing conditions, expected injuries prevented, and costs. Consider impacts on users who depend on the affected roads, lanes, or sidewalks.</p></section></main>
