 effectiveness?","choices":["Congress could not directly collect taxes.","Congress could override presidential vetoes.","The Supreme Court had too many judges.","The Bill of Rights restricted trade."],"answer":0,"hint":"The national government lacked reliable revenue.","explanation":"Without direct taxing power, Congress struggled to fund national responsibilities."},{"id":"q18","cs":"CS 6 • Articles weaknesses","topic":"CS 6 • Articles weaknesses","prompt":"Why did Federalists emphasize the absence of a national executive under the Articles?","choices":["States had no governors.","There was no effective national officer to carry out congressional decisions.","Congress could enforce laws too aggressively. under the constitutional process in the question","Courts controlled the military."],"answer":1,"hint":"Think enforcement.","explanation":"The Articles lacked a national executive capable of administering and enforcing national policy."},{"id":"q19","cs":"CS 6 • Necessary and Proper","topic":"CS 6 • Necessary and Proper","prompt":"Congress creates a national agency to carry out a listed power. Which clause most supports that action?","choices":["Tenth Amendment","Article V","Necessary and Proper Clause","Bill of Rights as an action taken by public officials"],"answer":2,"hint":"Think implied powers.","explanation":"The Necessary and Proper Clause permits laws needed to execute enumerated powers."},{"id":"q20","cs":"CS 6 • Supremacy","topic":"CS 6 • Supremacy","prompt":"A valid federal law conflicts with a state law. Which rule resolves the conflict?","choices":["The state law always prevails.","Both laws automatically become void.","The governor chooses which law applies nationally. when applied through ordinary legal procedures","The valid federal law prevails under the Supremacy Clause."],"answer":3,"hint":"The Constitution calls federal law the supreme law of the land.","explanation":"The Supremacy Clause gives valid federal law priority over conflicting state l