ces of broad national authority."},{"id":"q25","cs":"CS 6 • Comparison","topic":"CS 6 • Comparison","prompt":"Which statement correctly compares the two sides?","choices":["Federalists favored stronger national authority; Anti-Federalists emphasized state power and written rights.","Both sides wanted to keep the Articles unchanged. within the government system described","Anti-Federalists favored national supremacy more strongly.","Federalists opposed ratification unless the presidency was removed."],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare their preferred balance of power.","explanation":"The central disagreement concerned national strength, state authority, and protection of liberty."},{"id":"q26","cs":"CS 6 • Outcome","topic":"CS 6 • Outcome","prompt":"Which result best describes the outcome of the ratification debate?","choices":["The Articles remained permanently in force.","The Constitution was ratified and a Bill of Rights was added soon afterward.","The Constitution was rejected by every state. under the constitutional process in the question","The presidency was removed before ratification."],"answer":1,"hint":"Both sides influenced the result.","explanation":"Ratification created the new government; the Bill of Rights addressed demands for explicit protections."},{"id":"q27","cs":"CS 7 • Formal amendment","topic":"CS 7 • Formal amendment","prompt":"Which action is a formal constitutional change?","choices":["Congress holding an oversight hearing","a president creating a cabinet tradition as an action taken by public officials","ratifying a new amendment through Article V","a court interpreting an existing clause"],"answer":2,"hint":"Formal change alters the written text.","explanation":"Article V amendment changes the Constitution itself."},{"id":"q28","cs":"CS 7 • Article V","topic":"CS 7 • Article V","prompt":"Which statement correctly describes one part of the amendment process?","choices":["The president may amend the Constitution by executive order. when applied through ordinary legal procedures","A simple majority of voters automatically changes the text.","The Supreme Court ratifies every amendment.","An amendment may be proposed by two-thirds of both houses of Congress."],"answer":3,"hint":"Think supermajorities.","explanation":"Article V requires broad agreement to propose and ratify amendments."},{"id":"q29","cs":"CS 7 • Article V","topic":"CS 7 • Article V","prompt":"Why is the formal amendment process intentionally difficult?","choices":["to require broad national agreement before changing the Constitution","to prevent any future constitutional change within the government system described","to give one branch total control","to allow states to ignore amendments"],"answer":0,"hint":"Difficulty promotes stability and consensus.","explanation":"Supermajority requirements balance adaptability with constitutional stability."},{"id":"q30","cs":"CS 7 • Amendments","topic":"CS 7 • Amendments","prompt":"Which amendment lowered the voting age to 18?","choices":["Tenth Amendment","Twenty-sixth Amendment","Nineteenth Amendment","Twenty-fourth Amendment"],"answer":1,"hint":"It was adopted during the Vietnam era.","explanation":"The Twenty-sixth Amendment protects voting rights for citizens age 18 and older."},{"id":"q31","cs":"CS 7 • Amendments","topic":"CS 7 • Amendments","prompt":"Which is the best example of an amendment expanding political participation?","choices":["the Necessary and Proper Clause as an action taken by public officials","Marbury v. Madison","the Nineteenth Amendment protecting women’s voting rights","a congressional oversight hearing"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look for a formal change involving suffrage.","explanation":"The Nineteenth Amendment constitutionally protected voting rights regardless of sex."},{"id":"q32","cs":"CS 7 • Judicial review","topic":"CS 7 • Judicial review","prompt":"What did Marbury v. Madison contribute to constitutional government?","choices":["It added the Bill of Rights.","It lowered the 