-const QUESTIONS=[{"id":"q01","cs":"CS 5 • Principles","topic":"CS 5 • Principles","prompt":"Which definition best matches popular sovereignty?","choices":["power comes from the people","government power belongs only to judges","states may ignore the Constitution","officials may act without limits"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who is the original source of political power?","explanation":"Popular sovereignty means the people are the source of government authority."},{"id":"q02","cs":"CS 5 • Principles","topic":"CS 5 • Principles","prompt":"Which definition best matches consent of the governed?","choices":["Congress approves every court ruling","citizens permit government to rule","states control all national powers","the president chooses all voters"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think permission from citizens.","explanation":"Legitimate government rests on the permission of the people."},{"id":"q03","cs":"CS 5 • Principles","topic":"CS 5 • Principles","prompt":"Which definition best matches limited government?","choices":["government has unlimited emergency power","only state governments must obey laws","government power is restricted by law","citizens may cancel any law individually"],"answer":2,"hint":"Constitutional boundaries are the key.","explanation":"Limited government restricts what public officials may do."},{"id":"q04","cs":"CS 5 • Principles","topic":"CS 5 • Principles","prompt":"Which definition best matches rule of law?","choices":["judges create any law they want","only voters must obey laws","the executive branch controls elections","everyone must obey the law"],"answer":3,"hint":"No person stands above the law.","explanation":"Rule of law requires both citizens and officials to follow established laws."},{"id":"q05","cs":"CS 5 • Principles","topic":"CS 5 • Principles","prompt":"Which definition best matches federalism?","choices":["national and state governments share power","power is divided among three national branches","all power belongs to local governments","courts hold all government power"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think levels of government.","explanation":"Federalism divides governing power between the national government and the states."},{"id":"q06","cs":"CS 5 • Principles","topic":"CS 5 • Principles","prompt":"Which definition best matches separation of powers?","choices":["national and state governments share power","government powers are split among branches","citizens vote directly on every law","states may overrule federal courts"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think branches, not levels.","explanation":"Separation of powers assigns major functions to different branches."},{"id":"q07","cs":"CS 5 • Principles","topic":"CS 5 • Principles","prompt":"Which definition best matches checks and balances?","choices":["each state receives the same population","courts write the federal budget","each branch can restrain the others","the president controls every branch"],"answer":2,"hint":"A veto and Senate confirmation are examples.","explanation":"Checks and balances keep any one branch from becoming too powerful."},{"id":"q08","cs":"CS 5 • Structure","topic":"CS 5 • Structure","prompt":"Which definition best matches legislative branch?","choices":["the branch that carries out federal laws","the branch that interprets laws","the level that issues local licenses","the branch that makes federal laws"],"answer":3,"hint":"Think Congress.","explanation":"Congress is the legislative branch and makes federal laws."},{"id":"q09","cs":"CS 5 • Structure","topic":"CS 5 • Structure","prompt":"Which definition best matches executive branch?","choices":["the branch that carries out and enforces federal laws","the branch that writes constitutional amendments alone within the government system described","the branch that interprets laws","the level that ratifies every treaty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think President and agencies.","explanation":"The executive branch carries out and enforces federal laws."},{"id":"q10","cs":"CS 5 • Structure","topic":"CS 5 • Structure","prompt":"Which definition best matches judicial branch?","choices":["the branch that collects all state taxes","the branch that interprets laws","the branch that commands Congress","the branch that proposes every amendment"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think federal courts.","explanation":"The judicial branch interprets laws and the Constitution."},{"id":"q11","cs":"CS 5 • Federalism","topic":"CS 5 • Federalism","prompt":"Which definition best matches reserved powers?","choices":["powers given only to Congress","powers shared by all three branches","powers kept by the states","powers held only during war"],"answer":2,"hint":"The Tenth Amendment is the clue.","explanation":"Reserved powers are not delegated to the United States and remain with states or the people."},{"id":"q12","cs":"CS 5 • Federalism","topic":"CS 5 • Federalism","prompt":"Which definition best matches Tenth Amendment?","choices":["the amendment creating judicial review","the amendment lowering the voting age","the amendment ending slavery","protects powers kept by the states"],"answer":3,"hint":"Its Roman numeral is X.","explanation":"The Tenth Amendment supports the federal system by recognizing reserved powers."},{"id":"q13","cs":"CS 6 • Ratification","topic":"CS 6 • Ratification","prompt":"Which definition best matches Federalists?","choices":["favored Constitution ratification","opponents who demanded state supremacy","delegates who wanted to keep the Articles unchanged","judges who opposed all national courts"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think support for the proposed Constitution.","explanation":"Federalists supported ratification and stronger national authority."},{"id":"q14","cs":"CS 6 • Ratification","topic":"CS 6 • Ratification","prompt":"Which definition best matches Anti-Federalists?","choices":["supporters of the Supremacy Clause","opposed Constitution ratification","writers who opposed state power","officials who wanted no amendments"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think fear of a too-powerful national government.","explanation":"Anti-Federalists opposed ratification without stronger protections for liberty and states."},{"id":"q15","cs":"CS 6 • Ratification","topic":"CS 6 • Ratification","prompt":"Which definition best matches ratification?","choices":["a court declaring a law unconstitutional","Congress investigating an agency","formal approval","the president rejecting a bill"],"answer":2,"hint":"Think official approval.","explanation":"Ratification is the formal approval needed for a constitution or amendment."},{"id":"q16","cs":"CS 6 • Ratification","topic":"CS 6 • Ratification","prompt":"Which definition best matches Bill of Rights?","choices":["the first ten articles creating Congress","all Supreme Court opinions","the list of powers denied to states only","the first ten amendments"],"answer":3,"hint":"Anti-Federalists demanded it.","explanation":"The Bill of Rights added written protections for individual freedoms."},{"id":"q17","cs":"CS 6 • National power","topic":"CS 6 • National power","prompt":"Which definition best matches Necessary and Proper Clause?","choices":["supports Congress’s implied powers","a rule requiring identical state laws","the amendment process","a ban on national courts"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is also called the elastic clause.","explanation":"The clause supports implied powers needed to execute Congress’s enumerated powers."},{"id":"q18","cs":"CS 6 • National power","topic":"CS 6 • National power","prompt":"Which definition best matches Supremacy Clause?","choices":["the president is above the courts","federal law overrides state law","states may cancel federal laws","Congress may change court verdicts"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think national law versus conflicting state law.","explanation":"The Supremacy Clause makes valid federal law the supreme law of the land."},{"id":"q19","cs":"CS 6 • National power","topic":"CS 6 • National power","prompt":"Which definition best matches enumerated powers?","choices":["powers invented by courts","powers kept only by states","specifically listed powers","powers used only by local government"],"answer":2,"hint":"Enumerated means listed.","explanation":"Enumerated powers are expressly granted, such as taxing and regulating interstate commerce."},{"id":"q20","cs":"CS 6 • National power","topic":"CS 6 • National power","prompt":"Which definition best matches implied powers?","choices":["powers forbidden by the Bill of Rights","powers reserved only to states","powers used before the Constitution","powers inferred from listed powers"],"answer":3,"hint":"Think Necessary and Proper Clause.","explanation":"Implied powers help Congress carry out its enumerated responsibilities."},{"id":"q21","cs":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","topic":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","prompt":"Which definition best matches formal amendment?","choices":["an official constitutional change","a tradition followed by presidents","a Supreme Court explanation","an agency regulation"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think proposal and ratification.","explanation":"A formal amendment changes the written Constitution through Article V."},{"id":"q22","cs":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","topic":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","prompt":"Which definition best matches Article V?","choices":["the article establishing the presidency","the amendment process","the clause establishing federal supremacy","the amendment reserving state powers"],"answer":1,"hint":"It answers how the text is officially changed.","explanation":"Article V establishes the formal amendment process."},{"id":"q23","cs":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","topic":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","prompt":"Which definition best matches judicial review?","choices":["Congress watching executive agencies","states approving amendments","courts review constitutionality","the president vetoing legislation"],"answer":2,"hint":"Think Marbury v. Madison.","explanation":"Judicial review lets courts evaluate government action against the Constitution."},{"id":"q24","cs":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","topic":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","prompt":"Which definition best matches Marbury v. Madison?","choices":["the case ending school segregation","the law ending employment discrimination","the amendment lowering the voting age","the case establishing judicial review"],"answer":3,"hint":"This 1803 case involved Chief Justice Marshall.","explanation":"Marbury v. Madison established the principle of judicial review."},{"id":"q25","cs":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","topic":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","prompt":"Which definition best matches legislation?","choices":["laws enacted by Congress","customs that are never written","court decisions that amend Article V","state refusals to follow federal law"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think statutes such as civil-rights laws.","explanation":"Congress uses legislation to turn broad constitutional authority into specific policies."},{"id":"q26","cs":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","topic":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","prompt":"Which definition best matches legislative oversight?","choices":["the Supreme Court reviewing a conviction","Congress monitors the executive branch","states ratifying an amendment","the president nominating a judge"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think a congressional hearing.","explanation":"Oversight is an informal practice Congress uses to check administration of the laws."},{"id":"q27","cs":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","topic":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","prompt":"Which definition best matches Civil Rights Act of 1964?","choices":["an amendment lowering the voting age","a case creating judicial review","bans discrimination in employment","a law creating the Articles of Confederation"],"answer":2,"hint":"Think desegregation and employment equality.","explanation":"The act used congressional power to enforce civil-rights protections."},{"id":"q28","cs":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","topic":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","prompt":"Which definition best matches Voting Rights Act of 1965?","choices":["a clause expanding implied powers","a decision approving segregation","an amendment creating the Senate","targets unfair voting barriers"],"answer":3,"hint":"Think literacy tests and unequal access to the ballot.","explanation":"The act strengthened federal protection of voting rights."},{"id":"q29","cs":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","topic":"CS 7 • Constitutional change","prompt":"Which definition best matches living document?","choices":["the Constitution adapts over time","the Constitution automatically expires","officials may ignore the written text","states may rewrite federal laws"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think change over time using lawful methods.","explanation":"The Constitution endures while its application develops through recognized methods of change."},{"id":"q30","cs":"State Test Language","topic":"State Test Language","prompt":"Which definition best matches adequately?","choices":["quickly and without discussion","enough to meet the need","according to an old tradition","equally between every group"],"answer":1,"hint":"If something is adequate, it is enough for its purpose.","explanation":"In a released question, “adequately protect” means protect well enough to meet the need."},{"id":"q31","cs":"State Test Language","topic":"State Test Language","prompt":"Which definition best matches excessive?","choices":["required by the Constitution","shared equally by two governments","more than reasonable or necessary","reduced from an earlier amount"],"answer":2,"hint":"Think too much—not simply strong or important.","explanation":"In a released question, “excessive government power” means more government power than is reasonable or acceptable."},{"id":"q32","cs":"State Test Language","topic":"State Test Language","prompt":"Which definition best matches diminished?","choices":["formally approved by voters","divided into separate branches","expanded beyond its previous limit","reduced or weakened"],"answer":3,"hint":"Think made smaller or 