-const CONFIG={"mode":"review","type":"Comprehensive Review","block":4,"cs":"CS 9–11","title":"Reconstruction, Suffrage, and Presidency Review","description":"Apply CS 9–11 through scenarios, comparison, cause and effect, and constitutional reasoning.","practice":"Ohio OST-style application, analysis, and cause-and-effect reasoning.","assignmentKey":"gov-phase2-block4-review-2026-v2"};
-const QUESTIONS=[{"id":"q01","cs":"CS 9 • Thirteenth Amendment","topic":"CS 9 • Thirteenth Amendment","prompt":"A state attempts to legally restore slavery. Which amendment most directly prohibits this action?","choices":["Thirteenth Amendment","Fourteenth Amendment","Nineteenth Amendment","Twenty-second Amendment"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think abolition.","explanation":"The Thirteenth Amendment abolished slavery and involuntary servitude, except as criminal punishment."},{"id":"q02","cs":"CS 9 • Fourteenth Amendment","topic":"CS 9 • Fourteenth Amendment","prompt":"A child is born in Ohio to parents who are not U.S. citizens. Which amendment establishes the child’s U.S. citizenship?","choices":["Thirteenth Amendment","Fourteenth Amendment","Fifteenth Amendment","Twenty-sixth Amendment"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think birthright citizenship.","explanation":"The Citizenship Clause recognizes persons born in the United States as citizens."},{"id":"q03","cs":"CS 9 • Fourteenth Amendment","topic":"CS 9 • Fourteenth Amendment","prompt":"A state takes a person’s property without notice or a fair hearing. Which protection is most directly involved?","choices":["Fifteenth Amendment voting protection","Nineteenth Amendment suffrage","Fourteenth Amendment due process","Twenty-second Amendment term limits"],"answer":2,"hint":"The state skipped fair procedures.","explanation":"The Fourteenth Amendment prohibits states from depriving life, liberty, or property without due process."},{"id":"q04","cs":"CS 9 • Fourteenth Amendment","topic":"CS 9 • Fourteenth Amendment","prompt":"A state law gives legal protection to one racial group but denies it to another. Which clause is most directly involved?","choices":["Supremacy Clause","Necessary and Proper Clause","Establishment Clause","Equal Protection Clause"],"answer":3,"hint":"Think equal treatment under state law.","explanation":"The Fourteenth Amendment requires states to provide equal protection to all persons."},{"id":"q05","cs":"CS 9 • Fifteenth Amendment","topic":"CS 9 • Fifteenth Amendment","prompt":"A state refuses to register citizens because of their race. Which amendment is most directly violated?","choices":["Fifteenth Amendment","Thirteenth Amendment","Nineteenth Amendment","Twenty-third Amendment"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think race and voting.","explanation":"The Fifteenth Amendment forbids denial of voting rights based on race, color, or previous servitude."},{"id":"q06","cs":"CS 9 • Reconstruction","topic":"CS 9 • Reconstruction","prompt":"Which three amendments are collectively called the Reconstruction Amendments?","choices":["First, Second, and Third","Thirteenth, Fourteenth, and Fifteenth","Nineteenth, Twenty-fourth, and Twenty-sixth","Twelfth, Twenty-second, and Twenty-fifth"],"answer":1,"hint":"They were adopted after the Civil War.","explanation":"Amendments 13–15 ended slavery and added citizenship, equality, and voting protections."},{"id":"q07","cs":"CS 9 • Reconstruction","topic":"CS 9 • Reconstruction","prompt":"Which sequence correctly describes the Reconstruction Amendments?","choices":["protect speech → bear arms → ban searches","women’s suffrage → ban poll taxes → voting age 18 as an action taken by public officials","abolish slavery → define citizenship and equality → protect voting regardless of race","separate ballots → D.C. electors → succession"],"answer":2,"hint":"Follow Amendments 13, 14, and 15 in order.","explanation":"The amendments moved from freedom, to citizenship/equality, to political participation."},{"id":"q08","cs":"CS 9 • Continued struggle","topic":"CS 9 • Continued struggle","prompt":"Why did the Reconstruction Amendments not produce immediate equality?","choices":["The amendments restored slavery.","The amendments eliminated citizenship.","The amendments prohibited African American voting. when applied through ordinary legal procedures","Jim Crow laws and discriminatory practices continued segregation and restricted rights."],"answer":3,"hint":"Constitutional text did not erase resistance and discriminatory enforcement.","explanation":"Jim Crow and other practices undermined constitutional protections for generations."},{"id":"q09","cs":"CS 9 • Jim Crow","topic":"CS 9 • Jim Crow","prompt":"A state requires racially separate public facilities. Which historical system does this represent?","choices":["Jim Crow segregation","the Electoral College","presidential succession","birthright citizenship"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think enforced racial separation after Reconstruction.","explanation":"Jim Crow laws enforced segregation despite the Fourteenth Amendment."},{"id":"q10","cs":"CS 9 • Analysis","topic":"CS 9 • Analysis","prompt":"Which conclusion is best supported by the history of Reconstruction and Jim Crow?","choices":["An amendment always produces instant equality.","Constitutional protections may require enforcement and continued struggle to become reality.","States automatically follow every amendment without conflict. under the constitutional process in the question","Voting rights alone ended segregation."],"answer":1,"hint":"Compare the legal changes with what happened afterward.","explanation":"The gap between constitutional promise and lived equality persisted."},{"id":"q11","cs":"CS 10 • Fifteenth Amendment","topic":"CS 10 • Fifteenth Amendment","prompt":"Which voting-rights amendment prohibits racial discrimination in voting?","choices":["Nineteenth Amendment","Twenty-fourth Amendment","Fifteenth Amendment","Twenty-sixth Amendment"],"answer":2,"hint":"This amendment was part of Reconstruction.","explanation":"The Fifteenth Amendment protects voting regardless of race, color, or previous servitude."},{"id":"q12","cs":"CS 10 • Nineteenth Amendment","topic":"CS 10 • Nineteenth Amendment","prompt":"A state refuses to let women vote solely because they are women. Which amendment applies?","choices":["Fifteenth Amendment","Twenty-fourth Amendment","Twenty-sixth Amendment","Nineteenth Amendment"],"answer":3,"hint":"Think women’s suffrage.","explanation":"The Nineteenth Amendment prohibits denying voting rights based on sex."},{"id":"q13","cs":"CS 10 • Twenty-fourth Amendment","topic":"CS 10 • Twenty-fourth Amendment","prompt":"A citizen is told to pay a fee before voting in a federal election. Which amendment applies?","choices":["Twenty-fourth Amendment","Fifteenth Amendment","Nineteenth Amendment","Twenty-sixth Amendment"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fee is a poll tax.","explanation":"The Twenty-fourth Amendment prohibits poll taxes in federal elections."},{"id":"q14","cs":"CS 10 • Twenty-sixth Amendment","topic":"CS 10 • Twenty-sixth Amendment","prompt":"A state sets the voting age at 21 and refuses to register a 19-year-old citizen. Which amendment applies?","choices":["Nineteenth Amendment","Twenty-sixth Amendment","Twenty-fourth Amendment","Twenty-second Amendment"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think voting age 18.","explanation":"The Twenty-sixth Amendment protects voting rights for citizens 18 and older."},{"id":"q15","cs":"CS 10 • Poll taxes","topic":"CS 10 • Poll taxes","prompt":"Why did poll taxes disenfranchise many poor citizens?","choices":["They required voters to own newspapers. as an action taken by public officials","They limited presidents to two terms.","Voting required money that some eligible citizens could not afford.","They changed Electoral College ballots."],"answer":2,"hint":"Focus on the financial barrier.","explanation":"Poll taxes conditioned voting on payment and were used to suppress African American voters."},{"id":"q16","cs":"CS 10 • Suffrage","topic":"CS 10 • Suffrage","prompt":"Which set contains only suffrage-expanding amendments emphasized in CS 10?","choices":["13th, 14th, 22nd, and 25th","1st, 2nd, 4th, and 10th","12th, 22nd, 23rd, and 25th","15th, 19th, 24th, and 26th"],"answer":3,"hint":"Think race, sex, poll tax, and age.","explanation":"CS 10 emphasizes four amendments that removed voting barriers."},{"id":"q17","cs":"CS 10 • Cause and effect","topic":"CS 10 • Cause and effect","prompt":"Which cause-and-effect relationship is correct?","choices":["Poll taxes restricted poor voters, so the Twenty-fourth Amendment prohibited them in federal elections.","The Nineteenth Amendment created poll taxes, so the Fifteenth repealed it. within the government system described","The Twenty-sixth raised the voting age, so youth voting fell.","The Fifteenth established segregation, so Jim Crow ended."],"answer":0,"hint":"Match a barrier with the amendment addressing it.","explanation":"The Twenty-fourth Amendment removed a financial barrier to federal voting."},{"id":"q18","cs":"CS 10 • Comparison","topic":"CS 10 • Comparison","prompt":"How are the Fifteenth and Nineteenth Amendments similar?","choices":["Both establish presidential term limits. under the constitutional process in the question","Both prohibit denying voting rights based on a personal characteristic.","Both regulate Electoral College ballots.","Both address presidential disability."],"answer":1,"hint":"One concerns race; the other concerns sex.","explanation":"Both extended suffrage by prohibiting a form of discrimination."},{"id":"q19","cs":"CS 10 • Application","topic":"CS 10 • Application","prompt":"Which amendment most directly expanded the electorate during the Vietnam era?","choices":["Thirteenth Amendment","Twenty-second Amendment","Twenty-sixth Amendment","Twenty-third Amendment"],"answer":2,"hint":"“Old enough to fight, old enough to vote.”","explanation":"The Twenty-sixth Amendment lowered the voting age to 18."},{"id":"q20","cs":"CS 11 • Electoral College","topic":"CS 11 • Electoral College","prompt":"How is a state’s number of electoral votes determined?","choices":["its population divided by two when applied through ordinary legal procedures","the number of state judges","the governor’s decision","its U.S. representatives plus its two senators"],"answer":3,"hint":"Combine House and Senate representation.","explanation":"Each state receives electors equal to its total congressional delegation."},{"id":"q21","cs":"CS 11 • Electoral College","topic":"CS 11 • Electoral College","prompt":"What must a presidential candidate normally win to secure the presidency?","choices":["a majority of electoral votes","every state’s popular vote","a unanimous Senate vote","two-thirds of the House"],"answer":0,"hint":"More than half is required.","explanation":"The Electoral College requires a majority of appointed electoral votes."},{"id":"q22","cs":"CS 11 • Federalism","topic":"CS 11 • Federalism","prompt":"Why does the Electoral College reflect federalism?","choices":["Federal judges select the president. under the constitutional process in the question","Presidential selection is organized through electoral votes allocated to states.","Local mayors appoint electors nationally.","Congress ignores state representation."],"answer":1,"hint":"Think national selection through state units.","explanation":"The system combines a national office with state-based electoral votes."},{"id":"q23","cs":"CS 11 • Twelfth Amendment","topic":"CS 11 • Twelfth Amendment","prompt":"Electors cast separate ballots for president and vice president. Which amendment created this procedure?","choices":["Twenty-second Amendment","Twenty-third Amendment","Twelfth Amendment","Twenty-fifth Amendment"],"answer":2,"hint":"Think separate ballots.","explanation":"The Twelfth Amendment revised Electoral College voting after early tie problems."},{"id":"q24","cs":"CS 11 • Twelfth Amendment","topic":"CS 11 • Twelfth Amendment","prompt":"What problem was the Twelfth Amendment designed to reduce?","choices":["presidents serving too many terms when applied through ordinary legal procedures when applied through ordinary legal procedures","D.C. lacking electoral votes","presidential disability","Electoral ties and confusion caused by undifferentiated votes for president and vice president"],"answer":3,"hint":"The original process did not separate the two offices on b