-{title:"Part III — Federalists vs. Anti-Federalists",reading:`<h3>Simplified Arguments</h3><p><b>Federalist No. 10 — James Madison:</b> If government is too weak, groups with special interests could take over and harm the rights of others. A stronger republic can better control these groups.</p><p><b>Federalist No. 51 — James Madison:</b> Each branch of government should be able to stop the others from becoming too powerful. This way, liberty is protected because no single branch can dominate.</p><p><b>Brutus No. 1 — Anti-Federalist:</b> The Constitution gives too much power to the national government. Over time, the states will lose their independence, and people’s rights will be threatened.</p><p><b>Cato No. 3 — Anti-Federalist:</b> The President has too much military power and could use an army against the people, just like a king.</p>`,questions:[
