’s veto power."},{"id":"q15","cs":"CS 13 • Impeachment","topic":"CS 13 • Impeachment","prompt":"The House formally accuses a federal civil officer of misconduct. What action is this?","choices":["pardon","confirmation","impeachment","judicial review"],"answer":2,"hint":"Formal accusation occurs in the House.","explanation":"Impeachment is the House’s formal charge."},{"id":"q16","cs":"CS 13 • Removal","topic":"CS 13 • Removal","prompt":"After a House impeachment, which body conducts the trial that can lead to removal?","choices":["Supreme Court","Cabinet","Electoral College","Senate"],"answer":3,"hint":"The other chamber tries the case.","explanation":"The Senate tries impeachments and may convict and remove."},{"id":"q17","cs":"CS 13 • Appointments","topic":"CS 13 • Appointments","prompt":"The president nominates a cabinet secretary. Which branch checks this appointment?","choices":["Senate through confirmation","Supreme Court through appeal","House through a veto","states through ratification"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think advice and consent.","explanation":"Senate confirmation checks presidential appointments."},{"id":"q18","cs":"CS 13 • Treaties","topic":"CS 13 • Treaties","prompt":"The president negotiates a treaty. What check occurs before ratification?","choices":["House impeachment","Senate approval","Supreme Court nomination","presidential pardon"],"answer":1,"hint":"Treaties require Senate consent.","explanation":"The Senate checks executive treaty-making power."},{"id":"q19","cs":"CS 13 • Judges","topic":"CS 13 • Judges","prompt":"Who nominates federal judges?","choices":["House of Representatives","Supreme Court","president","state governors collectively"],"answer":2,"hint":"This is an executive check involving the judiciary.","explanation":"The president nominates federal judges; the Senate confirms."},{"id":"q20","cs":"CS 13 • Judges","topic":"CS 13 • Judges","prompt":"Why can the president not remove a federal judge merely for an unfavorable decision?","c