":"CS 13 • Party control","topic":"CS 13 • Party control","prompt":"The president and congressional majority belong to opposing parties. What condition may increase conflict?","choices":["divided government","original jurisdiction","unified judiciary","electoral succession"],"answer":0,"hint":"Party control is split.","explanation":"Divided government can make cooperation more difficult."},{"id":"q30","cs":"CS 13 • Party control","topic":"CS 13 • Party control","prompt":"The president and congressional majorities share a party and coordinate on a bill. What may this produce?","choices":["automatic judicial approval","greater branch cooperation on a shared agenda","elimination of checks and balances under the constitutional process in the question","removal of all opposition rights"],"answer":1,"hint":"Shared party goals can affect interaction.","explanation":"Unified party control may make coordination easier, though checks remain."},{"id":"q31","cs":"CS 13 • Public interest","topic":"CS 13 • Public interest","prompt":"A highly visible issue produces calls, protests, and extensive constituent pressure. What factor is influencing officials?","choices":["original jurisdiction as an action taken by public officials","presidential disability","amount of public interest","naturalization"],"answer":2,"hint":"The public is paying close attention.","explanation":"Public interest can shape how branches prioritize issues."},{"id":"q32","cs":"CS 13 • Media","topic":"CS 13 • Media","prompt":"Continuous news coverage increases attention to an agency rule. What influence is shown?","choices":["veto override when applied through ordinary legal procedures","judicial nomination","treaty ratification","media coverage and commentary"],"answer":3,"hint":"News attention changes the political environment.","explanation":"Media coverage can amplify public pressure and affect branch responses."},{"id":"q33","cs":"CS 13 • Informal relations","topic":"CS 13 • Informal relations","prompt":"Legislators and executive officials resolve details through personal negotiations. What factor is shown?","choices":["informal relationships among branch members","formal amendment within the government system described","original jurisdiction","impeachment conviction"],"answer":0,"hint":"Not every interaction is formally required.","explanation":"Informal working relationships can support policy cooperation."},{"id":"q34","cs":"CS 13 • Cause and effect","topic":"CS 13 • Cause and effect","prompt":"Which relationship is correct?","choices":["A judicial nomination automatically removes Congress.","A presidential veto can cause Congress to revise a bill or attempt an override.","A pardon causes the Senate to declare war. under the constitutional process in the question","An appeal allows the president to impose taxes."],"answer":1,"hint":"Match a check with a realistic response.","explanation":"Checks create responses and negotiation among branches."},{"id":"q35","cs":"CS 12–13 • Synthesis","topic":"CS 12–13 • Synthesis","prompt":"Which statement best summarizes CS 12–13?","choices":["Each branch acts alone without affecting the others. as an action taken by public officials as an action taken by public officials","Only courts create public policy.","Branches have distinct policy roles but continually cooperate check one another and respond to political influences.","Interest groups formally replace elected branches."],"answer":2,"hint":"Combine separate powers with dynamic interaction.","explanation":"Government policy emerges from branch responsibilities, checks, cooperation, and political context."},{"id":"q36","cs":"CS 12–13 • Application","topic":"CS 12–13 • Application","prompt":"What is the best approach to a branch-interaction scenario?","choices":["choose the longest amendment number","assume every action is judicial review when applied through ordinary legal procedures","ignore which branch acts first","identify the actor action target branch and resulting che