-const QUESTIONS=[{"id":"q01","cs":"CS 5 • Popular sovereignty","topic":"CS 5 • Popular sovereignty","prompt":"Citizens replace several officeholders in an election. Which principle is most directly illustrated?","choices":["popular sovereignty","federal supremacy","judicial review","legislative oversight"],"answer":0,"hint":"Focus on who chooses the officials.","explanation":"Elections demonstrate that government authority begins with the people."},{"id":"q02","cs":"CS 5 • Popular sovereignty","topic":"CS 5 • Popular sovereignty","prompt":"Which statement best expresses consent of the governed?","choices":["Government is legitimate whenever it is powerful. under the constitutional process in the question","Government is legitimate because the people authorize it.","Only judges may approve government action.","State governments do not need public support."],"answer":1,"hint":"Consent means permission.","explanation":"Consent of the governed connects legitimate authority to the people’s approval."},{"id":"q03","cs":"CS 5 • Limited government","topic":"CS 5 • Limited government","prompt":"A court blocks an official from using a power not granted by law. Which principle is being enforced?","choices":["popular sovereignty","federalism","limited government","ratification"],"answer":2,"hint":"The official faces a legal boundary.","explanation":"Limited government restricts officials to lawful, constitutional authority."},{"id":"q04","cs":"CS 5 • Rule of law","topic":"CS 5 • Rule of law","prompt":"A governor is prosecuted under the same criminal law that applies to other citizens. What does this show?","choices":["separation of powers","implied powers","state supremacy","rule of law"],"answer":3,"hint":"No official is above the law.","explanation":"Rule of law requires government officials as well as citizens to obey the law."},{"id":"q05","cs":"CS 5 • Federalism","topic":"CS 5 • Federalism","prompt":"The national government regulates interstate commerce while a state sets teacher-license rules. Which principle explains this division?","choices":["federalism","checks and balances","judicial review","popular sovereignty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think levels of government.","explanation":"Federalism divides authority between the national and state governments."},{"id":"q06","cs":"CS 5 • Federalism","topic":"CS 5 • Federalism","prompt":"Which action is most clearly a power generally reserved to a state?","choices":["coining money under the constitutional process in the question","issuing driver licenses","declaring war","negotiating treaties"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look for a familiar state service.","explanation":"Licensing is a common example of a reserved state power."},{"id":"q07","cs":"CS 5 • Federalism","topic":"CS 5 • Federalism","prompt":"Which constitutional provision most directly supports the idea of reserved state powers?","choices":["Supremacy Clause","Necessary and Proper Clause","Tenth Amendment","Article V"],"answer":2,"hint":"The amendment number is X.","explanation":"The Tenth Amendment reserves undelegated powers to states or the people."},{"id":"q08","cs":"CS 5 • Separation of powers","topic":"CS 5 • Separation of powers","prompt":"Congress writes a law, the president enforces it, and courts interpret it. Which principle is illustrated?","choices":["popular sovereignty when applied through ordinary legal procedures","federalism","ratification","separation of powers"],"answer":3,"hint":"Different functions belong to different branches.","explanation":"Separation of powers divides legislative, executive, and judicial functions."},{"id":"q09","cs":"CS 5 • Checks and balances","topic":"CS 5 • Checks and balances","prompt":"The president vetoes a bill passed by Congress. This is an example of—","choices":["checks and balances","reserved powers within the government system described","popular sovereignty","formal amendment"],"answer":0,"hint":"One branch is limiting another.","explanation":"The veto is an executive check on the legislative branch."},{"id":"q10","cs":"CS 5 • Checks and balances","topic":"CS 5 • Checks and balances","prompt":"The Senate votes on whether to confirm a presidential nominee. What principle is at work?","choices":["state supremacy","checks and balances","judicial review","consent of the governed only"],"answer":1,"hint":"The legislative branch is checking an executive choice.","explanation":"Senate confirmation is a legislative check on appointment power."},{"id":"q11","cs":"CS 5 • Checks and balances","topic":"CS 5 • Checks and balances","prompt":"A federal court declares a law unconstitutional. Which two ideas are most directly involved?","choices":["federalism and ratification","popular sovereignty and state supremacy as an action taken by public officials","judicial review and checks and balances","oversight and amendment"],"answer":2,"hint":"A court is reviewing and limiting another branch.","explanation":"Judicial review allows courts to check unconstitutional government action."},{"id":"q12","cs":"CS 5 • Principles","topic":"CS 5 • Principles","prompt":"Why did the framers combine separation of powers with checks and balances?","choices":["to let each branch act without limits when applied through ordinary legal procedures","to give state courts control of Congress","to eliminate conflict among branches","to divide power and prevent any branch from dominating"],"answer":3,"hint":"The goal was protection against concentrated power.","explanation":"Dividing power is stronger when each branch can also restrain the others."},{"id":"q13","cs":"CS 5 • Application","topic":"CS 5 • Application","prompt":"Congress passes a law, but the president vetoes it and Congress considers an override. Which principle is the best answer?","choices":["checks and balances","federalism","rule of law only","popular sovereignty only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the interaction between branches.","explanation":"Veto and override powers are reciprocal checks."},{"id":"q14","cs":"CS 5 • Application","topic":"CS 5 • Application","prompt":"Which situation best illustrates limited government rather than rule of law?","choices":["A mayor receives the same traffic fine as any citizen. under the constitutional process in the question","The Constitution prevents Congress from exercising a power it was not granted.","A jury applies a law to a defendant.","Police enforce a valid law equally."],"answer":1,"hint":"Ask whether the example is about boundaries on power or equal obedience to law.","explanation":"Limited government focuses on restricting the amount and type of power government may use."},{"id":"q15","cs":"CS 6 • Federalists","topic":"CS 6 • Federalists","prompt":"Which position would a Federalist most likely support in 1788?","choices":["Keep the Articles unchanged because Congress is already strong. as an action taken by public officials","Reject every national court.","Ratify the Constitution to create a more effective national government.","Give states complete supremacy over national law."],"answer":2,"hint":"Federalists believed the Articles made national government too weak.","explanation":"Federalists supported ratification and stronger national authority."},{"id":"q16","cs":"CS 6 • Anti-Federalists","topic":"CS 6 • Anti-Federalists","prompt":"Which concern would an Anti-Federalist most likely raise?","choices":["Congress will remain unable to tax under the Constitution. when applied through ordinary legal procedures","The president will have no power to enforce laws.","The national judiciary will be eliminated.","The proposed national government might threaten states and individual liberty."],"answer":3,"hint":"Think fear of concentrated central power.","explanation":"Anti-Federalists feared that a strong national government could endanger liberty and state authority."},{"id":"q17","cs":"CS 6 • Articles weaknesses","topic":"CS 6 • Articles weaknesses","prompt":"Which weakness of the Articles of Confederation most clearly limited national effectiveness?","choices":["Congress could not directly collect taxes.","Congress could override presidential vetoes.","The Supreme Court had too many judges.","The Bill of Rights restricted trade."],"answer":0,"hint":"The national government lacked reliable revenue.","explanation":"Without direct taxing power, Congress struggled to fund national responsibilities."},{"id":"q18","cs":"CS 6 • Articles weaknesses","topic":"CS 6 • Articles weaknesses","prompt":"Why did Federalists emphasize the absence of a national executive under the Articles?","choices":["States had no governors.","There was no effective national officer to carry out congressional decisions.","Congress could enforce laws too aggressively. under the constitutional process in the question","Courts controlled the military."],"answer":1,"hint":"Think enforcement.","explanation":"The Articles lacked a national executive capable of administering and enforcing national policy."},{"id":"q19","cs":"CS 6 • Necessary and Proper","topic":"CS 6 • Necessary and Proper","prompt":"Congress creates a national agency to carry out a listed power. Which clause most supports that action?","choices":["Tenth Amendment","Article V","Necessary and Proper Clause","Bill of Rights as an action taken by public officials"],"answer":2,"hint":"Think implied powers.","explanation":"The Necessary and Proper Clause permits laws needed to execute enumerated powers."},{"id":"q20","cs":"CS 6 • Supremacy","topic":"CS 6 • Supremacy","prompt":"A valid federal law conflicts with a state law. Which rule resolves the conflict?","choices":["The state law always prevails.","Both laws automatically become void.","The governor chooses which law applies nationally. when applied through ordinary legal procedures","The valid federal law prevails under the Supremacy Clause."],"answer":3,"hint":"The Constitution calls federal law the supreme law of the land.","explanation":"The Supremacy Clause gives valid federal law priority over conflicting state law."},{"id":"q21","cs":"CS 6 • Ratification","topic":"CS 6 • Ratification","prompt":"What did Anti-Federalists gain as part of the ratification settlement?","choices":["a promise to add a Bill of Rights","abolition of the presidency within the government system described","complete state supremacy","removal of national courts"],"answer":0,"hint":"They demanded written protections.","explanation":"The promise and later adoption of the Bill of Rights helped answer Anti-Federalist objections."},{"id":"q22","cs":"CS 6 • Ratification","topic":"CS 6 • Ratification","prompt":"What was the main purpose of the Federalist Papers?","choices":["to demand a return to British rule under the constitutional process in the question","to persuade people to support ratification of the Constitution","to replace the Bill of Rights","to abolish Congress under the Articles"],"answer":1,"hint":"They defended the proposed Constitution.","explanation":"Hamilton, Madison, and Jay wrote essays supporting ratification."},{"id":"q23","cs":"CS 6 • Ratification","topic":"CS 6 • Ratification","prompt":"Hamilton, Madison, and Jay are most closely connected to which publication?","choices":["Brutus I only","the Declaration of Independence","The Federalist Papers","the Northwest Ordinance"],"answer":2,"hint":"These essays defended ratification.","explanation":"The three authors wrote the Federalist Papers under the name Publius."},{"id":"q24","cs":"CS 6 • Ratification","topic":"CS 6 • Ratification","prompt":"Why did Anti-Federalists object to the Necessary and Proper and Supremacy Clauses?","choices":["They believed the clauses made Congress too weak. when applied through ordinary legal procedures","They wanted federal laws to override all rights.","They opposed adding any state governments.","They feared the clauses would expand national power at the expense of states."],"answer":3,"hint":"Focus on central power versus state authority.","explanation":"Anti-Federalists saw the clauses as possible sources of broad national authority."},{"id":"q25","cs":"CS 6 • Comparison","topic":"CS 6 • Comparison","prompt":"Which statement correctly compares the two sides?","choices":["Federalists favored stronger national authority; Anti-Federalists emphasized state power and written rights.","Both sides wanted to keep the Articles unchanged. within the government system described","Anti-Federalists favored national supremacy more strongly.","Federalists opposed ratification unless the presidency was removed."],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare their preferred balance of power.","explanation":"The central disagreement concerned national strength, state authority, and protection of liberty."},{"id":"q26","cs":"CS 6 • Outcome","topic":"CS 6 • Outcome","prompt":"Which result best describes the outcome of the ratification debate?","choices":["The Articles remained permanently in force.","The Constitution was ratified and a Bill of Rights was added soon afterward.","The Constitution was rejected by every state. under the constitutional process in the question","The presidency was removed before ratification."],"answer":1,"hint":"Both sides influenced the result.","explanation":"Ratification created the new government; the Bill of Rights addressed demands for explicit protections."},{"id":"q27","cs":"CS 7 • Formal amendment","topic":"CS 7 • Formal amendment","prompt":"Which action is a formal constitutional change?","choices":["Congress holding an oversight hearing","a president creating a cabinet tradition as an action taken by public officials","ratifying a new amendment through Article V","a court interpreting an existing clause"],"answer":2,"hint":"Formal change alters the written text.","explanation":"Article V amendment changes the Constitution itself."},{"id":"q28","cs":"CS 7 • Article V","topic":"CS 7 • Article V","prompt":"Which statement correctly describes one part of the amendment process?","choices":["The president may amend the Constitution by executive order. when applied through ordinary legal procedures","A simple majority of voters automatically changes the text.","The Supreme Court ratifies every amendment.","An amendment may be proposed by two-thirds of both houses of Congress."],"answer":3,"hint":"Think supermajorities.","explanation":"Article V requires broad agreement to propose and ratify amendments."},{"id":"q29","cs":"CS 7 • Article V","topic":"CS 7 • Article V","prompt":"Why is the formal amendment process intentionally difficult?","choices":["to require broad national agreement before changing the Constitution","to prevent any future constitutional change within the government system described","to give one branch total control","to allow states to ignore amendments"],"answer":0,"hint":"Difficulty promotes stability and consensus.","explanation":"Supermajority requirements balance adaptability with constitutional stability."},{"id":"q30","cs":"CS 7 • Amendments","topic":"CS 7 • Amendments","prompt":"Which amendment lowered the voting age to 18?","choices":["Tenth Amendment","Twenty-sixth Amendment","Nineteenth Amendment","Twenty-fourth Amendment"],"answer":1,"hint":"It was adopted during the Vietnam era.","explanation":"The Twenty-sixth Amendment protects voting rights for citizens age 18 and older."},{"id":"q31","cs":"CS 7 • Amendments","topic":"CS 7 • Amendments","prompt":"Which is the best example of an amendment expanding political participation?","choices":["the Necessary and Proper Clause as an action taken by public officials","Marbury v. Madison","the Nineteenth Amendment protecting women’s voting rights","a congressional oversight hearing"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look for a formal change involving suffrage.","explanation":"The Nineteenth Amendment constitutionally protected voting rights regardless of sex."},{"id":"q32","cs":"CS 7 • Judicial review","topic":"CS 7 • Judicial review","prompt":"What did Marbury v. Madison contribute to constitutional government?","choices":["It added the Bill of Rights.","It lowered the voting age.","It gave states complete supremacy.","It established judicial review."],"answer":3,"hint":"The case strengthened the Court’s interpretive role.","explanation":"Marbury is associated with courts judging whether government acts are constitutional."},{"id":"q33","cs":"CS 7 • Interpretation","topic":"CS 7 • Interpretation","prompt":"How can Supreme Court decisions change constitutional government without changing the written text?","choices":["They interpret how constitutional provisions apply to laws and new situations.","They secretly rewrite Article V.","They automatically create amendments.","They allow Congress to ignore the Constitution. within the government system described"],"answer":0,"hint":"Interpretation changes application, not wording.","explanation":"Court decisions develop the meaning and reach of constitutional provisions."},{"id":"q34","cs":"CS 7 • Civil rights","topic":"CS 7 • Civil rights","prompt":"Brown v. Board of Education changed constitutional interpretation by holding that—","choices":["Congress cannot regulate commerce","racial segregation in public schools violated equal protection","the president alone may amend the Constitution under the constitutional process in the question","states may deny voting rights freely"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think equal protection and schools.","explanation":"Brown applied the Fourteenth Amendment to reject segregated public education."},{"id":"q35","cs":"CS 7 • Legislation","topic":"CS 7 • Legislation","prompt":"How did the Civil Rights Act of 1964 add detail to constitutional principles?","choices":["It created judicial review.","It ratified the Fourteenth Amendment. as an action taken by public officials","It prohibited discrimination in employment and major public settings.","It established the electoral college."],"answer":2,"hint":"Think a federal statute enforcing equality.","explanation":"Congress used legislation to give practical effect to civil-rights principles."},{"id":"q36","cs":"CS 7 • Legislation","topic":"CS 7 • Legislation","prompt":"The Voting Rights Act of 1965 most directly targeted—","choices":["the presidential veto when applied through ordinary legal procedures","state driver licensing","Supreme Court nominations","racially discriminatory barriers to voting such as literacy tests"],"answer":3,"hint":"Focus on access to the ballot.","explanation":"The act strengthened federal enforcement against discriminatory voting practices."},{"id":"q37","cs":"CS 7 • Oversight","topic":"CS 7 • Oversight","prompt":"Congress calls agency officials to testify about how a law is being enforced. What method of constitutional change or development is shown?","choices":["informal practice through legislative oversight","formal amendment within the government system described","state ratification","judicial review"],"answer":0,"hint":"A congressional hearing monitors the executive branch.","explanation":"Oversight is an informal practice that shapes how constitutional responsibilities operate."},{"id":"q38","cs":"CS 7 • Oversight","topic":"CS 7 • Oversight","prompt":"Why is legislative oversight important to checks and balances?","choices":["It lets Congress reverse every court ruling. under the constitutional process in the question","It lets Congress monitor executive administration and demand accountability.","It allows agencies to write amendments.","It removes the need for elections."],"answer":1,"hint":"Think monitoring implementation.","explanation":"Oversight helps the legislative branch check how the executive carries out laws."},{"id":"q39","cs":"CS 7 • Change over time","topic":"CS 7 • Change over time","prompt":"Which list includes all four methods emphasized in CS 7?","choices":["elections, treaties, political parties, and taxes","vetoes, pardons, campaigns, and federalism","amendments, Supreme Court interpretation, legislation, and informal practice","state laws, local ordinances, juries, and cabinet meetings"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look for formal text change plus three ways government develops without rewriting the text.","explanation":"CS 7 emphasizes amendments, judicial interpretation, congressional legislation, and informal practices such as oversight."},{"id":"q40","cs":"CS 5–7 • Synthesis","topic":"CS 5–7 • Synthesis","prompt":"Which sequence best shows how a constitutional principle can develop over time?","choices":["A governor cancels the Constitution and replaces it with a statute. when applied through ordinary legal procedures","Congress ignores court rulings and removes elections.","A state vetoes a federal amendment after ratification.","A broad equality principle is interpreted by courts and enforced through later legislation."],"answer":3,"hint":"Combine interpretation and legislation.","explanation":"Constitutional principles gain practical meaning through court decisions and laws enacted under constitutional authority."}];
